{
  "id": "Compound::DB00762 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Irinotecan, a topoisomerase inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of neutropenic colitis, which is subsequently linked to hematologic cancer through the action of Idarubicin or Dasatinib. The shared mechanism of action—topoisomerase inhibition—establishes a foundational link between Irinotecan and the treatment of hematologic cancer. The evidence suggests that Irinotecan may contribute to the development of neutropenic colitis, which in turn could serve as a mediator in the progression to hematologic malignancies. The presence of a common ancestor as an organooxygen compound further supports the biochemical pathway linking these entities. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Irinotecan to influence hematologic cancer through a multi-step mechanism involving inflammation and cellular damage. The observed scores and shared biological features suggest that the relationship between Irinotecan and hematologic cancer is supported by a mechanistic framework involving topoisomerase inhibition and its downstream effects.",
